    {"id": 1, "claim": "The average translational kinetic energy of particles in a substance is directly proportional to its absolute temperature (Kelvin).", "evidence": "Kinetic Molecular Theory: $KE_{avg} = \\frac{3}{2}k_B T$.", "weight": 0.30, "depends_on": []},
    {"id": 2, "claim": "The approximate temperatures of the substances are: (A) Wax ~20-25°C, (B) Water in ice water ~0°C, (C) Steam ~100°C, (D) Ice cube ~0°C.", "evidence": "Standard phase change temperatures and room temperature definitions. An ice cube in a glass of tea is at the melting point (0°C) if it is melting, or potentially lower if just removed from a freezer, but never higher than the surrounding liquid water at equilibrium.", "weight": 0.25, "depends_on": []},
    {"id": 3, "claim": "Options (B) and (D) have the lowest temperatures among the choices.", "evidence": "0°C is lower than ~20°C and ~100°C.", "weight": 0.15, "depends_on": [2]},
    {"id": 4, "claim": "At the same temperature, particles in a solid (ice) have lower average kinetic energy than particles in a liquid (water) because the solid's energy is partitioned into vibrational modes, whereas the liquid has translational and rotational degrees of freedom contributing to kinetic energy.", "evidence": "Statistical mechanics: Equipartition theorem implies more degrees of freedom for liquids/gases at the same T, or simply that constrained motion in solids limits the magnitude of particle velocity compared to free motion in liquids.", "weight": 0.25, "depends_on": [1, 3]},
    {"id": 5, "claim": "Therefore, the ice cube (solid at 0°C) has particles with the lowest kinetic energy compared to the water in ice water (liquid at 0°C).", "evidence": "Combining temperature comparison and state-of-matter kinetic energy differences.", "weight": 0.05, "depends_on": [4]}
  ],
